---
import FeaturePage from '../layouts/FeaturePage.astro';
import FeatureActions from '../components/FeatureActions.astro';
import ProductMedia from '../components/ProductMedia.astro';
import workspace from '../assets/hero.webp';
import { links } from '../config';
---

<FeaturePage
  title="Purr — A Local-First API Client"
  description="Purr stores workspaces locally, requires no account, keeps secrets out of Git-friendly files, and encrypts sensitive response data before writing it to disk."
  name="Local-first API client"
  variant="local-page"
>
  <header class="container feature-hero local-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow emerald">LOCAL-FIRST API CLIENT</p>
    <h1>
      Your API client should not need <span class="muted">your API data.</span>
    </h1>
    <p class="feature-lead">
      When I started building Purr, the default assumption was simple: the requests belong on your
      machine.
    </p>
    <p>
      You should not need an account just to send an HTTP request, and your API history, cookies,
      secrets, and response data should not quietly become somebody else’s cloud data.
    </p>
    <p class="local-principle">Purr is local-first by default.</p>
    <FeatureActions />
  </header>
  <section class="container ownership-intro">
    <div class="ownership-note">
      <p class="eyebrow emerald">NO ACCOUNT REQUIRED</p>
      <h2>
        Open the app
        <br />
        and use it.
      </h2>
      <p>There is no required signup flow before you can create a workspace or send a request.</p>
      <p>
        Purr does not need an account to decide whether you are allowed to use your own local API
        client. Your workspace exists locally and works locally.
      </p>
    </div>
    <ProductMedia
      src={workspace}
      alt="Purr local Riverside Community workspace with saved requests, a response and the Saved locally status"
      caption="Your requests, in your local workspace."
    />
  </section>
  <section class="container feature-editorial">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow emerald">YOUR WORKSPACE IS YOURS</p>
      <h2>Requests are stored on your machine.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-prose">
      <p>Workspaces, requests, variables, and project configuration are stored locally.</p>
      <p>
        The goal is to make them easy to understand, move, back up, and eventually keep with the
        project that uses them. This is also why Git-friendly workspace files are important to Purr.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container storage-story">
    <div class="feature-heading-row">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow emerald">GIT-FRIENDLY DOES NOT MEAN SECRETS IN GIT</p>
        <h2>
          Share the request,
          <br />
          <span class="muted">not the credentials.</span>
        </h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        Some workspace data should be portable. Secrets should not. Purr separates sensitive values
        from the files intended to live in your project or repository.
      </p>
    </div>
    <!-- Conceptual storage diagram; no workspace-file or encryption-settings capture was supplied. -->
    <div
      class="storage-boundary"
      role="group"
      aria-label="Portable workspace definitions and separate local encrypted storage"
    >
      <div>
        <span class="storage-label">PROJECT / GIT-FRIENDLY FILES</span>
        <h3>
          <code>workspace.yaml</code>
        </h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Requests</li>
          <li>Configuration</li>
          <li>Variable definitions</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Portable request definitions</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="storage-label">ON YOUR MACHINE / ENCRYPTED</span>
        <h3>Local encrypted storage</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Secrets</li>
          <li>Cookies</li>
          <li>Sensitive values</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Sensitive values stay separate</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="storage-caption">
      Authentication secrets, cookies, and other sensitive values are not written into the YAML
      workspace files that are meant to be committed to Git.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="container encryption-story">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow emerald">ENCRYPTED BEFORE IT HITS DISK</p>
      <h2>Local does not have to mean plaintext.</h2>
      <p>
        API responses can contain much more sensitive data than the request itself. User records,
        tokens, internal service responses, debugging payloads, or production data can easily end up
        in local history.
      </p>
      <p>
        Purr encrypts sensitive persisted data before storing it on disk. That includes the data
        that should not simply sit around as readable application files.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="storage-pipeline">
      <figcaption>ON YOUR MACHINE</figcaption>
      <ol class="feature-flow vertical-flow" aria-label="Response encryption before local storage">
        <li>
          Response <code>{'{ … }'}</code>
        </li>
        <li class="encrypt-step">
          Encrypt <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          Local storage <code>••••••</code>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>Encrypted before it reaches disk.</p>
    </figure>
  </section>
  <section class="container response-history">
    <p class="eyebrow emerald">RESPONSES CAN STAY ENCRYPTED</p>
    <h2>Response history should not become a plaintext archive.</h2>
    <p>
      Purr can store response bodies encrypted locally rather than leaving previous API responses
      readable on disk. The client still needs to be able to show you the response when you open it,
      but persisted response data is encrypted before storage.
    </p>
    <a class="text-link" href="/docs/encryption/">
      Read about encryption →
    </a>
  </section>
  <section class="container open-source-story">
    <div class="source-mark" aria-hidden="true">
      &lt;/&gt;<span>MIT / PUBLIC CORE</span>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow emerald">OPEN SOURCE WHERE TRUST MATTERS</p>
      <h2>You can inspect the client you give your credentials to.</h2>
      <p>
        Purr’s public core is available under the MIT license. For an API client, I think that
        matters.
      </p>
      <p>
        You are putting authentication headers, cookies, internal endpoints, and sometimes
        production data into the application. You should be able to inspect how the client handles
        them instead of relying only on a privacy statement.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some integrations and commercial functionality may live outside the public core, but the
        client itself is being developed in the open.
      </p>
      <a class="text-link" href={links.github}>
        View Purr on GitHub ↗
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container feature-editorial">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow emerald">LOCAL-FIRST IS NOT OFFLINE-ONLY</p>
      <h2>
        Local ownership first.
        <br />
        <span class="muted">Integrations when you ask for them.</span>
      </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-prose">
      <p>
        Purr can connect to external systems when a feature requires it — for example a{' '}
        <a href="/tracing/">tracing provider</a> you explicitly configure.
      </p>
      <p>
        Local-first does not mean pretending the network does not exist. It means Purr does not
        require its own cloud service to hold your requests just so the desktop client can work.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container data-story">
    <p class="eyebrow emerald">WHY THIS MATTERS FOR AN API CLIENT</p>
    <h2>The data inside a developer tool can be more sensitive than the tool itself.</h2>
    <ul class="sensitive-data-list">
      {[
        'Authorization tokens',
        'Cookies',
        'Private service URLs',
        'Request bodies',
        'Customer data',
        'Internal API responses',
        'Debugging information',
      ].map((value) => (
        <li>{value}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p>
      For Purr, keeping that data local was not an extra privacy mode added later. It shaped how the
      workspace and storage were designed.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="container feature-end">
    <h2>
      Your requests can stay
      <br />
      <span class="muted">on your machine.</span>
    </h2>
    <p>
      No required account, local workspaces, encrypted sensitive storage, and source you can
      inspect.
    </p>
    <FeatureActions />
    <a class="text-link" href="/docs/encryption/">
      Read about encryption →
    </a>
  </section>
</FeaturePage>
